        <briefdescription>
<para>Reads the system clock. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Isolates the platform-specific access to the system clock, used by <computeroutput><ref refid="classmicro__os__plus_1_1micro__test__plus_1_1detail_1_1stopwatch" kindref="compound">stopwatch</ref></computeroutput> and by the deprecated <computeroutput><ref refid="classmicro__os__plus_1_1micro__test__plus_1_1detail_1_1timestamp" kindref="compound">timestamp</ref></computeroutput> class. The clock is monotonic on POSIX; without a clock, <computeroutput>ts</computeroutput> is left unchanged, so the callers clear it first when a zero value must mean that the clock was not read.</para>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="out">ts</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The <computeroutput>timespec</computeroutput> where the reading is stored. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="par"><title>Returns</title><para>Nothing.</para>
</simplesect>
On Windows, the current time is captured via <computeroutput>timespec_get()</computeroutput> with <computeroutput>TIME_UTC</computeroutput>. On POSIX platforms with <computeroutput>CLOCK_MONOTONIC</computeroutput> defined, <computeroutput>clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)</computeroutput> is used to obtain a monotonic reading. On platforms where neither macro is defined, <computeroutput>ts</computeroutput> is left unchanged. </para>
        </detaileddescription>
